  data: {
    LLM: 'Mark penalty tiles (3-suit and double-0) so they are easy to spot; the game feels different from Hearts—moon shots are more frequent due to smaller deck and every tile belonging to two suits. Follow suit when possible; avoid taking tricks with penalty tiles.\n\nUse double-0: leading it as blank guarantees winning the trick; leading it as double (if another double is out) can dump it. Lead low blanks to try to force 0-0 onto someone else, but beware of helping a moon-shot. The 3-3 is high in the penalty suit but can fall to a higher double.\n\nWhen passing: passing high/doubles and penalty tiles to your left helps their moon-shot; holding doubles and penalty tiles can be the best defence. Defending against moon-shots is hard—penalty tiles can fall to other suits. Balance dumping points vs blocking the shooter.',
    Player: 'Mark penalty tiles (3-suit and double-0) so they are easy to spot; the game feels different from Hearts—moon shots are more frequent due to smaller deck and every tile belonging to two suits. Follow suit when possible; avoid taking tricks with penalty tiles.\n\nUse double-0: leading it as blank guarantees winning the trick; leading it as double (if another double is out) can dump it. Lead low blanks to try to force 0-0 onto someone else, but beware of helping a moon-shot. The 3-3 is high in the penalty suit but can fall to a higher double.\n\nWhen passing: passing high/doubles and penalty tiles to your left helps their moon-shot; holding doubles and penalty tiles can be the best defence. Defending against moon-shots is hard—penalty tiles can fall to other suits. Balance dumping points vs blocking the shooter.',
